WEBVTT

00:00:00.022 --> 00:00:03.067
Now we connect the grounded prompt to a language model.

00:00:03.067 --> 00:00:09.812
The example uses the OpenAI Python client, but the important idea is provider independence.

00:00:09.812 --> 00:00:17.247
The same retrieval pipeline can work with a hosted API or with an OpenAI-compatible local endpoint.

00:00:17.247 --> 00:00:24.857
We keep the API key and model configuration in environment variables rather than writing credentials into the

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:30.457
notebook. For this kind of support or policy RAG, we also use a low temperature.

00:00:30.457 --> 00:00:33.387
We are not asking the model to be creative.

00:00:33.387 --> 00:00:36.702
We want a stable answer that follows the evidence.

00:00:36.702 --> 00:00:40.712
Remember that generation is only one step in the pipeline.

00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:44.907
The model receives the question together with retrieved context.

00:00:44.907 --> 00:00:50.937
If retrieval is correct and the prompt is clear, generation has a much easier job.

00:00:50.937 --> 00:00:56.160
Never commit API keys to GitHub or include them directly in student files.
